": "01:01:52", "text": "The Allnighter on FBi Radio 94.5 Sydney with us. We are going to do it."}, {"start": "01:02:00", "end": "01:02:46", "text": "The first three letters in Ngu means inner, Ngu means emotional. That's the definition of Aum for Wal-Pi people and Central Australian people. Ngu-Ra, something that runs like a power source that keeps your body going. And that's your old Ngu-Ra-Kul history, your language history, your sermon history, your skin name ceremony within that tribe. Your jurisdiction."}, {"start": "01:03:16", "end": "01:05:28", "text": "To exist in the homeland of the kangaroo, you have to understand. To understand it, we'll try and teach you to soar really high like the wet stand. It's like reading a book. The behavior of animals, the behavior of... Once you master that, you adapt really well. Law from this country, it shapes us who we are. They call this crown land, I sometimes wonder what, what crown, ask you to find out the original crown of this country. You are like a milvery, fulfilling your duty of care, we call it. The grand result is you have fulfilled something. Your fulfillment of carrying your country, giving back to your country. And that's all the knowledge for your own well-being from this country, to give it back to people. In doing so, you already got the original crown. The emus crown. I am warm, but I'm a, what up?"}, {"start": "01:05:40", "end": "01:07:19", "text": "Yeah, the original crown is a southern cross here, because it's sitting on the emu's head. Yeah, to us, that's the whole ngurakulu. It's a reminder that we mustn't be like a little star tripping away. That's why the little stars is the homeland. or this continent and to learn all that one will expand your learning how you should live in this country and look firstly you must learn all these high ties in the law the skin name the land the ceremony and the language To live in the woman and the kangaroo, you have to understand the immune."}, {"start": "01:07:32", "end": "01:08:24", "text": "Your country will know you. Satsang with Mooji"}, {"start": "05:04:44", "end": "05:05:22", "text": "Love, Thomas Sarko throne, Shabung, You see sunlight doesn't expand like we've always been drawing the sun coming up the mountains. Now it shoots straight up towards death. During this year in ceremony, when the men sing, women dance, flicking their finger like that. So they get to expand the light going this way horizontally."}, {"start": "05:05:50", "end": "05:06:38", "text": "First, you have to grow like a tree, straight up. That means, yeah, everything is born unto you. Everything around you, yeah, gonna be you. Embrace them while you can. Learn about them, because they'll guide you how you should live, because you're going to have to adapt to this harsh country and enjoy its richness, I guess, with that knowledge. Because if you don't, yeah, your birch ride is stripped from you and your life falls short. It's really hard country out here."}, {"start": "05:08:19", "end": "05:09:05", "text": "That's what I mean by negative and positive consequences of the law. It makes or breaks you, Haka and Albert, it's just the way the country is built. Or created, that's all."}], "fbi_json": [{"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "00:00:00"}, "track": {"title": "Stardust", "artists": [{"name": "Anna von Hausswolff"}]}, "isPremiere": false, "_confidence": "mid"}, {"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "00:12:07"}, "track": {"title": "Other Right Hand of the Lord", "artists": [{"name": "Spike F**k"}]}, "isPremiere": false, "_confidence": "mid"}, {"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "01:01:00"}, "track": {"title": "NGURRA KURLU (HOME)", "artists": [{"name": "Crown and Country"}, {"name": "Jerry Jangala Patrick"}, {"name": "MWNCI & Wanta Jampijinpa Pawu-Kurlpurlurnu"}]}, "isPremiere": false, "_confidence": "high"}, {"timestamp": {"enabled": true, "formatted": "01:09:00"}, "track": {"title": "Show Me Love (Original)", "artists": [{"n